This add-on requires the packages from the HSL library as given by the target
libhsl3 in the root Makefile.

HSL packages can be obtained free of charge by academics for research and
teaching purposes. See the website: http://hsl.rl.ac.uk

The following modules for interfacing to HSL library routines are available:
  1) Computing the condition number of a matrix (including the system matrix).
     (hsl_mc75)
  2) Iterative solvers using algebraic multigrid preconditioners
     (hsl_solve_mi20) and diagonal preconditioners (hsl_solve_diag). 
  3) Special iterative solvers for the Stokes system based on the book of Elman
     et al. (2005). (hsl_solve1_mi20, hsl_solve2_mi20, hsl_solve3_mi20).


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

NOTE from the manual of hsl_mi20: 

"The matrix A must have positive diagonal entries and (most of) the
off-diagonal entries must be negative (the diagonal should be large
compared to the sum of the off-diagonals)".


Performance of solve_system_mi20
================================

Intel(R) Core(TM)2 Duo CPU     T9300  @ 2.50GHz

eps_rel=1e-7, eps_abs=0, move_matrix=.true.

2D Poisson system with biquadratic shape functions (Q2):

mesh   numits  matrix   total_mem  build  setup  solve  total  mem_ma57  build+solve_ma57

200^2    4       41M     153M       .35s   .34s   .67s  1.36s    119M        1.6s
400^2    4      163M     414M      1.4s   1.4s   2.7s   5.5s     565M        9.7s
600^2    4      367M     830M      3.2s   3.1s   6.2s  12.5s     1.4G        27s
1000^2   4      1.0G     2.2G      8.9s   9.0s  18.2s  36.1s     4.1G    

3D Poisson system with biquadratic shape functions (Q2):

mesh   numits  matrix   total_mem  build  setup  solve  total  mem_ma57  build+solve_ma57

10^3    3                          0.14s  0.08s  0.05s  0.27s    27M          0.40s
15^3    3                          0.48s  0.34s  0.29s  1.11s   199M          5.0s
20^3    3       85M      134M      1.12s  0.87s  0.64s  2.63s   803M         32.2s
25^3    3      114M      209M      2.22s  1.85s  1.35s  5.42s   1.6G         91s
30^3    3      201M      340M      3.83s  3.31s  2.39s  9.53s   3.9G 
40^3    3      489M      796M      9.0s   8.2s   5.7s  23.9s   15.8G 
60^3    3      1.7G      2.6G     30.4s  29.4s  21.0s  80.9s      


NOTES:
 - Default parameters for the AMG preconditioner (hsl_mi20) are used.
   See [1] for a possible increase in performance using other parameters.
   [1] J. Boyle, M. D. Mihajlovic and J. A. Scott, HSL MI20: an efficient AMG
       preconditioner, RAL-TR-2007-021, December 2007. Download pdf from
       http://www.numerical.rl.ac.uk/reports/reports.html
 - Used the default iterative solver (BiCGSTAB). For this problem the system
   matrix is symmetric and positive definite. Therefore the CG iterative
   solver is also applicable, and might be preferable. GMRES can also be used.
 - Run 'gnuplot gnu.gpi' for a plot of the data.
 - Pardiso is more efficient for 3D problems than MA57.
 - A mesh of 60^3 means about 120^3=1.728*10^6 degrees of freedom.
 - AMG scales linearly with the number of degrees of freedom for CPU and memory.
 - Scaling ma57 up to 60^3 (see gnuplot gnu.gpi) would need a CPU time of more
   than 2 hours and memory of around 120G! This means AMG is 100 times more
   efficient regarding CPU and 50 times regarding memory for this resolution!
 - Scaling AMG to 100^3 we need around 10G and 400s.
 - The build/setup/solve phases are of the same order of magnitude for large
   3D problems. This means that parallelization of all three phases is
   necessary to get a significant speedup.
 - If the matrix remains constant in a time-dependent scheme we can achieve
   a 4x speedup by skipping the build and setup.

